# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# gagoline.co.il runs on Cloudflare Pages (project `gagoline`), cut over 2026-08-02. Pages reads
# this file from the deployed output; `public/*` is copied into `out/` by the static export, so it
# ships with every deploy via <hub>/ops/deploy-site.ps1.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE CAN AND CANNOT DO HERE — measured 2026-09-01, not assumed:
#
#   CANNOT — override the five headers Cloudflare injects at THIS zone. gagoline is one of the four
#            zones carrying the fleet header Transform Rules (with netomazganim, visionsecurity and
#            dangates). Proof: the live response returns them Title-Cased with
#            `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=15552000`, which is the zone's value, not one set
#            here. Sibling sites whose zones have NO such rules (myhomeplumber, thermoleak) serve
#            their own `_headers` values lowercase — the casing is how you tell the two apart.
#
#   CAN    — set anything the zone does not send. CSP and Cache-Control are both in that category,
#            and gagoline was serving NO Content-Security-Policy at all before this file.
#
# So: to change HSTS or Permissions-Policy here, edit the ZONE. Editing this file will not do it.

/*
  # Mirrored from the zone so the intended policy is visible in the repo and so the site stays
  # covered if the zone rules are ever removed. The zone currently wins; these are not the live
  # values you are reading in `curl -sI`.
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=15552000

  # REPORT-ONLY, and it must stay that way until violations have been observed on the live site.
  # The blocker for enforcement is the inline GTM snippet: a static export cannot mint a
  # per-request nonce, so enforcing needs either 'unsafe-inline' or a hash allowlist regenerated
  # whenever the snippet changes.
  #
  # Origins are the ones actually referenced by the build, not a template: GTM, the Web3Forms
  # endpoint the lead form POSTs to, the WhatsApp handoff, and the shared imgquarry media host.
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://www.googletagmanager.com https://www.google-analytics.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https://imgquarry.com https://www.googletagmanager.com https://www.google-analytics.com; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https://api.web3forms.com https://www.google-analytics.com https://www.googletagmanager.com; form-action 'self' https://api.web3forms.com; frame-src https://www.googletagmanager.com; frame-ancestors 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'

# Content-addressed: the filename changes whenever the bytes do, so this is safe forever.
/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# NO Cache-Control ON HTML, and that is a measured decision, not an omission.
#
# Three findings, all from probing the live site rather than reasoning about the docs:
#
#   1. The rule here used to be `/*.html`, and it matched NOTHING. next.config.mjs sets
#      trailingSlash: true, so every live HTML address is directory-style — /pricing/,
#      /blog/<slug>/ — with no ".html" for the glob to hit. 42 of 43 indexed routes fell through
#      to the Pages default; only "/" carried the value, because "/" had its own rule. The header
#      meant to cover the whole site covered exactly one page.
#
#   2. Moving it into the /* block DID match every route — and broke the static assets, because
#      Cloudflare Pages MERGES the directives of every matching rule rather than letting the more
#      specific one win. Measured on a forced cache MISS, a hashed chunk came back as
#      "public, max-age=31536000, s-maxage=3600, must-revalidate, immutable" — a content-addressed
#      file that can safely be held forever, told to revalidate hourly. must-revalidate alongside
#      immutable is a contradiction the edge should never have been handed.
#
#   3. And it buys nothing anyway. Even on "/", where the header was correct all along, repeated
#      requests kept returning cf-cache-status: DYNAMIC. Cloudflare Pages does not honour s-maxage
#      for HTML without an explicit Cache Rule at the zone.
#
# So: zero benefit, real harm. HTML gets no Cache-Control from this file until a zone Cache Rule
# exists to make it mean something — that is an owner action in the dashboard, like every other
# zone setting (CLAUDE.md §12). Do not "fix" this by adding the rule back; finding 2 is why.
